WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.146
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.146 --> 00:00:05.912
Building Non-Person Identity.

3
00:00:05.912 --> 00:00:10.037
This is Chapter 8: Conditional
Access for workload identities.

4
00:00:10.037 --> 00:00:12.587
A stolen client secret
works from anywhere,

5
00:00:12.587 --> 00:00:15.987
unless something says where
the workload is allowed to be.

6
00:00:15.987 --> 00:00:20.850
So that place is written down, reviewed,
and enforced one ring at a time.

7
00:00:20.850 --> 00:00:23.613
We'll start from Learn's
own sample policy,

8
00:00:23.613 --> 00:00:28.812
see why Graph would refuse it as published,
then walk the scope file, the checks,

9
00:00:28.812 --> 00:00:30.763
the plan, enforcement by ring,

10
00:00:30.763 --> 00:00:34.500
the sign-in reader and the
alert, two labs and the dangers.

11
00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:40.020
One note before we begin: the code is
illustrative, tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:45.282
Why does this chapter matter?

13
00:00:45.282 --> 00:00:48.057
Because a stolen secret
works from anywhere,

14
00:00:48.057 --> 00:00:52.257
and Conditional Access is how you
pin a workload to its own networks.

15
00:00:52.257 --> 00:00:55.126
Learn is short and firm
about what it can do:

16
00:00:55.126 --> 00:01:00.207
block from outside known ranges, or on
risk, and blocking is the only control.

17
00:01:00.207 --> 00:01:04.460
Four limits then decide the whole
scope: you need the premium license,

18
00:01:04.460 --> 00:01:08.340
only single-tenant apps are
covered, managed identities aren't,

19
00:01:08.340 --> 00:01:11.995
and a policy assigned through
a group simply isn't enforced.

20
00:01:11.995 --> 00:01:16.610
For this estate, everything goes
to the worldwide Graph endpoint.

21
00:01:20.360 --> 00:01:23.086
The program's design
comes from the workbook's

22
00:01:23.086 --> 00:01:25.597
Conditional Access library: two policies.

23
00:01:25.597 --> 00:01:29.997
One blocks workloads from anywhere
except their named egress ranges.

24
00:01:29.997 --> 00:01:34.160
The other blocks them when Microsoft's
risk detection marks them high.

25
00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.329
They roll out report-only
first, then ring by ring,

26
00:01:37.329 --> 00:01:39.785
and they're never deleted on rollback.

27
00:01:39.785 --> 00:01:41.273
Two decisions frame it.

28
00:01:41.273 --> 00:01:44.972
The licensing decision covers
the top-tier single-tenant apps.

29
00:01:44.972 --> 00:01:48.293
The enforcement decision
is the security chief's:

30
00:01:48.293 --> 00:01:51.936
enforce, wait longer, or
enforce with dated exclusions.

31
00:01:51.936 --> 00:01:54.998
And don't worry when the
risk policy reports nothing;

32
00:01:54.998 --> 00:01:58.220
that's normal until
something is actually risky.

33
00:02:01.970 --> 00:02:03.845
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

34
00:02:03.845 --> 00:02:07.288
Learn's page gives portal
steps for both kinds of policy,

35
00:02:07.288 --> 00:02:11.629
says to save them report-only first,
tells you where to read the results,

36
00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:15.895
and ends with one sample policy in
JSON for the beta Graph endpoint.

37
00:02:15.895 --> 00:02:18.430
The repository searched Microsoft's sample

38
00:02:18.430 --> 00:02:21.307
repositories and found
nothing else to start from.

39
00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:25.954
So the page is pinned, unmodified, with
checked hashes and a weekly watch,

40
00:02:25.954 --> 00:02:30.522
and its one JSON block is extracted
and sent to a fake Graph and to the

41
00:02:30.522 --> 00:02:35.290
program's own pre-merge check, exactly
as if someone had committed it.

42
00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.654
The sample's shape is right,

43
00:02:40.654 --> 00:02:44.102
and the code keeps it as the
model for the location policy.

44
00:02:44.102 --> 00:02:45.890
But the values aren't values.

45
00:02:45.890 --> 00:02:50.745
The state field is literally the text
"enabled or disabled", and the service

46
00:02:50.745 --> 00:02:55.440
principal ID is a placeholder in
brackets, so Graph refuses it outright.

47
00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:58.502
Worse, the easy ways to
fill it in are dangerous.

48
00:02:58.502 --> 00:03:03.377
One option covers every service
principal in the tenant, reviewed or not.

49
00:03:03.377 --> 00:03:07.252
Another trusts every network you've
marked trusted for people, too.

50
00:03:07.252 --> 00:03:11.695
The code allows only reviewed
object IDs and one dedicated,

51
00:03:11.695 --> 00:03:14.040
untrusted named location.

52
00:03:17.790 --> 00:03:21.615
The rest of the gaps are about what
a program needs around one policy.

53
00:03:21.615 --> 00:03:25.077
A field the stable API
marks required is missing.

54
00:03:25.077 --> 00:03:27.741
There's no JSON at all
for the risk policy.

55
00:03:27.741 --> 00:03:30.353
The results live only in the admin center,

56
00:03:30.353 --> 00:03:33.203
where a pipeline can't
keep them as evidence.

57
00:03:33.203 --> 00:03:36.690
There's no record of who's in
scope, who's out, and why.

58
00:03:36.690 --> 00:03:38.890
And there are no rings and no alert,

59
00:03:38.890 --> 00:03:42.977
so enforcement is all or nothing
and nobody hears about a block.

60
00:03:42.977 --> 00:03:47.730
So the verdict: keep the sample's shape,
and don't create it as published.

61
00:03:51.480 --> 00:03:52.705
What does the code add?

62
00:03:52.705 --> 00:03:57.512
Its own module, building on Chapter 1's
core, and unlike the earlier read paths,

63
00:03:57.512 --> 00:04:01.480
this one can write Conditional
Access, so it's careful about how.

64
00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:05.930
The two policies, the named location
and the alert are reviewed files.

65
00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:11.444
The scope file is the record Gate 5 asks
for: who's in, by object ID and ring,

66
00:04:11.444 --> 00:04:15.817
who's out and what covers them
instead, and the decisions.

67
00:04:15.817 --> 00:04:18.328
Each policy is rendered once per ring,

68
00:04:18.328 --> 00:04:23.960
so enforcing one ring changes one policy
and leaves the next one in report-only.

69
00:04:27.710 --> 00:04:32.473
There are two checks: one reads only
files and runs on every pull request,

70
00:04:32.473 --> 00:04:35.622
the other runs against
the tenant on every plan.

71
00:04:35.622 --> 00:04:40.512
Most of the nine rules come straight from
Learn's limits: assign by object ID,

72
00:04:40.512 --> 00:04:44.534
never by group; no managed
identities; single-tenant apps only;

73
00:04:44.534 --> 00:04:48.398
and an object ID that really
is the enterprise application.

74
00:04:48.398 --> 00:04:52.632
The program adds its own: the
licensing decision must be recorded,

75
00:04:52.632 --> 00:04:57.650
every policy file stays report-only and
block-only, egress ranges stay narrow,

76
00:04:57.650 --> 00:05:03.410
and enforcement needs the security chief's
decision and a reviewed sign-in summary.

77
00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.921
The plan compares the
files with the tenant,

78
00:05:09.921 --> 00:05:13.373
property by property, and
gives each policy one action.

79
00:05:13.373 --> 00:05:14.798
Two details matter.

80
00:05:14.798 --> 00:05:17.720
A new policy is always
created report-only,

81
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.561
even if its ring is approved for
enforcement; it turns on in the next run.

82
00:05:22.561 --> 00:05:26.972
And a policy someone made by hand in
the portal is reported, never deleted.

83
00:05:26.972 --> 00:05:30.785
Run as the read task, the client
simply can't send a write.

84
00:05:30.785 --> 00:05:34.335
Run as the apply task, it
refuses on any finding.

85
00:05:34.335 --> 00:05:36.908
Rollback follows the workbook's design:

86
00:05:36.908 --> 00:05:41.560
take the ring off the record, and
its policies go back to report-only.

87
00:05:45.310 --> 00:05:48.794
Turning a policy on is the moment
a production job can stop,

88
00:05:48.794 --> 00:05:51.116
so the code asks for five things at once,

89
00:05:51.116 --> 00:05:55.035
or the ring stays held in report-only
with the reason written down.

90
00:05:55.035 --> 00:05:57.985
The security chief's
decision must mark the ring.

91
00:05:57.985 --> 00:06:00.898
The change must explicitly
allow enforcement.

92
00:06:00.898 --> 00:06:04.422
A reviewed sign-in summary
must be cited by its hash.

93
00:06:04.422 --> 00:06:06.673
That summary must show the ring ready.

94
00:06:06.673 --> 00:06:09.711
And every earlier ring
must already be enforced.

95
00:06:09.711 --> 00:06:13.068
Exclusions are allowed,
but each one has an owner,

96
00:06:13.068 --> 00:06:18.390
a reason and an end date, and it drops
out by itself when that date arrives.

97
00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:25.430
The evidence comes from the
service principal sign-in logs,

98
00:06:25.430 --> 00:06:26.840
and there are three traps.

99
00:06:26.840 --> 00:06:30.077
Without the right filter you
only get interactive sign-ins.

100
00:06:30.077 --> 00:06:32.915
Without a Conditional
Access read permission,

101
00:06:32.915 --> 00:06:35.389
the policy results are silently left out.

102
00:06:35.389 --> 00:06:37.761
And without a special request header,

103
00:06:37.761 --> 00:06:41.127
the report-only results come
back as an unknown value.

104
00:06:41.127 --> 00:06:45.090
The reader handles all three,
and throws rather than guessing.

105
00:06:45.090 --> 00:06:47.215
It gives each principal a status,

106
00:06:47.215 --> 00:06:50.678
and a ring is ready when
everyone's clear for two weeks.

107
00:06:50.678 --> 00:06:55.130
Once enforced, a block alert
goes to security operations.

108
00:06:58.880 --> 00:06:59.930
Who's involved?

109
00:06:59.930 --> 00:07:01.317
Developers build it.

110
00:07:01.317 --> 00:07:04.029
Identity engineers own the policies.

111
00:07:04.029 --> 00:07:07.722
Application owners have workloads
in scope, and the security

112
00:07:07.722 --> 00:07:11.993
officer and security operations
review results and receive the alert.

113
00:07:11.993 --> 00:07:14.155
Three tasks split the permissions.

114
00:07:14.155 --> 00:07:17.305
Reading needs only policy
and application read.

115
00:07:17.305 --> 00:07:22.016
Reading sign-ins needs the audit log
permission plus the narrowest of the three

116
00:07:22.016 --> 00:07:26.655
ways to see policy results; Learn doesn't
rank them, so the chapter picked by name.

117
00:07:26.655 --> 00:07:30.633
Only the apply task holds the
write permission, and a test keeps

118
00:07:30.633 --> 00:07:35.270
every task's permissions equal to
its calls, with no delete anywhere.

119
00:07:39.020 --> 00:07:43.858
Lab 1 runs on a laptop, with the fixture's
date fixed at the end of October.

120
00:07:43.858 --> 00:07:48.053
First you run the tests on Learn's
sample as published, and read the one

121
00:07:48.053 --> 00:07:52.321
where the fake Graph refuses it,
because its state is just display text.

122
00:07:52.321 --> 00:07:54.470
Then list what each task needs.

123
00:07:54.470 --> 00:07:58.020
Then run the pre-merge check
on the repository's own files,

124
00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:01.495
which passes cleanly; a finding
would fail the pipeline.

125
00:08:01.495 --> 00:08:05.368
And then the instructive step: run
the same check on Learn's sample,

126
00:08:05.368 --> 00:08:10.610
and it reports five findings, one for each
of the dangers we walked through earlier.

127
00:08:14.360 --> 00:08:18.010
Next, plan against the fictional
tenant, sending nothing.

128
00:08:18.010 --> 00:08:22.773
The named location is missing its
IPv6 range, so it's an update.

129
00:08:22.773 --> 00:08:26.648
Ring 1's location policy
already exists and matches.

130
00:08:26.648 --> 00:08:28.061
Three policies are new.

131
00:08:28.061 --> 00:08:30.556
And a policy someone made in the portal,

132
00:08:30.556 --> 00:08:34.535
covering every service principal,
is reported but left alone.

133
00:08:34.535 --> 00:08:36.210
Then read the sign-ins.

134
00:08:36.210 --> 00:08:38.148
Ring 1 is clear and ready.

135
00:08:38.148 --> 00:08:42.807
In Ring 2, a deploy bot signed in from
an address outside the egress ranges,

136
00:08:42.807 --> 00:08:45.759
so it would be blocked,
and that ring isn't ready.

137
00:08:45.759 --> 00:08:48.830
Notice that no user
sign-ins come back at all.

138
00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.243
Now apply the plan to the fake, twice.

139
00:08:55.243 --> 00:08:59.120
The first run sends four requests,
and the second sends none,

140
00:08:59.120 --> 00:09:04.264
because everything already matches; and
every workload policy is report-only,

141
00:09:04.264 --> 00:09:07.192
while an unrelated user
policy is untouched.

142
00:09:07.192 --> 00:09:08.942
Then try to enforce Ring 1.

143
00:09:08.942 --> 00:09:11.614
Record the decision
and the summary's hash,

144
00:09:11.614 --> 00:09:16.193
and plan without the enforcement switch:
the ring is held, with the reason.

145
00:09:16.193 --> 00:09:20.087
Add the switch and the summary,
and the location policy turns on,

146
00:09:20.087 --> 00:09:23.381
while the brand-new risk
policy waits for the next run.

147
00:09:23.381 --> 00:09:26.720
Change one byte of the
summary, and it's held again.

148
00:09:30.470 --> 00:09:34.795
Lab 2 uses a test tenant with a
Premium trial, never production.

149
00:09:34.795 --> 00:09:39.364
You write a scope with one test app,
by its enterprise application ID,

150
00:09:39.364 --> 00:09:42.220
and an egress range of
exactly one address.

151
00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:44.820
A reading app plans with
read permissions only.

152
00:09:44.820 --> 00:09:47.728
Then you add the write
permission, apply twice,

153
00:09:47.728 --> 00:09:50.482
and confirm everything landed report-only.

154
00:09:50.482 --> 00:09:53.560
Generate sign-ins from inside
and outside the range,

155
00:09:53.560 --> 00:09:55.707
and read them with a separate reader.

156
00:09:55.707 --> 00:09:59.354
After two clear weeks, enforce
Ring 1 in its own change,

157
00:09:59.354 --> 00:10:02.171
and watch the outside sign-in get blocked.

158
00:10:02.171 --> 00:10:03.740
Then back it all out.

159
00:10:07.490 --> 00:10:09.924
Several of these dangers are quiet ones:

160
00:10:09.924 --> 00:10:14.940
a policy that looks configured and enforces
nothing, because the app was assigned

161
00:10:14.940 --> 00:10:19.440
through a group, or by the registration's
ID instead of the enterprise app's.

162
00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:22.623
Others are loud: switching
a policy straight on,

163
00:10:22.623 --> 00:10:25.153
so a workload that moved stops at once.

164
00:10:25.153 --> 00:10:29.725
And some hide in the evidence:
report-only results that never came back,

165
00:10:29.725 --> 00:10:33.352
so the summary looks clean, or
a block nobody hears about.

166
00:10:33.352 --> 00:10:36.829
Then the slow ones: a lapsed
license, so a fix can't ship,

167
00:10:36.829 --> 00:10:39.578
and a write permission left lying around.

168
00:10:39.578 --> 00:10:41.000
Each has a guard.

169
00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.100
Here's what done looks like.

170
00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:48.363
The licensing decision is recorded.

171
00:10:48.363 --> 00:10:51.157
Every in-scope app is
top-tier, single-tenant,

172
00:10:51.157 --> 00:10:55.039
listed by its enterprise
application ID, in exactly one ring,

173
00:10:55.039 --> 00:10:59.463
and everything out of scope names
the control that covers it instead.

174
00:10:59.463 --> 00:11:04.087
The egress location holds only the
workload ranges and isn't trusted.

175
00:11:04.087 --> 00:11:06.312
Every policy started report-only.

176
00:11:06.312 --> 00:11:08.981
Each ring was enforced in its own change,

177
00:11:08.981 --> 00:11:13.751
on two clean weeks of reviewed evidence,
with the alert already in place.

178
00:11:13.751 --> 00:11:17.362
Next, Chapter 9 takes
on AI agent identities.

179
00:11:17.362 --> 00:11:18.520
Thanks for watching.
